1. Start with this definition

“As an AWS Cloud Architect, my responsibility is to translate business and application requirements into a secure, scalable, highly available, resilient, and cost-optimized AWS architecture. I work across networking, compute, storage, databases, security, IAM, monitoring, automation, disaster recovery, governance, and operational processes.

I don't necessarily implement every component myself. My primary responsibility is to make the architectural decisions, define standards and patterns, review implementation, identify risks and dependencies, and ensure that the final solution meets business requirements.”

That opening immediately sounds more like an Architect than a senior AWS administrator.

2. Requirement Gathering

The architect's work starts before AWS services are selected.

I first understand:

Business requirements
What is the application?
Who are the users?
How critical is it?
What is the expected traffic?
What is the business impact if it goes down?
What are the compliance requirements?
What is the budget?
Is it global or regional?
Technical requirements

I identify:

Availability requirements
Performance requirements
Scalability requirements
Security requirements
RTO
RPO
Data residency
Integration requirements
Backup requirements
Monitoring requirements

For example:

“If the application requires 99.99% availability, 15-minute RPO and 1-hour RTO, I cannot simply deploy one EC2 instance in one Availability Zone. Those requirements directly influence my architecture.”

3. AWS Landing Zone / Account Architecture

At enterprise level, I don't normally put everything into one AWS account.

I design a multi-account architecture.

For example:

AWS Organization
│
├── Management Account
│
├── Security Account
│
├── Log Archive Account
│
├── Network Account
│
├── Shared Services Account
│
├── Development Account
│
├── Test Account
│
├── Production Account
│
└── DR Account

Then I define:

AWS Organizations
OUs
Service Control Policies
IAM Identity Center
Centralized logging
Security services
Guardrails
Account provisioning
Network connectivity
Billing structure

This is where AWS Control Tower / Landing Zone concepts come into the architecture.

The objective is:

Separate environments, reduce blast radius, enforce governance, and provide centralized security and operations.

