26. Your Role as a Cloud Architect

Given your Linux + AWS/Azure + Kubernetes + Terraform background, you can position yourself like this:

"My background started with Linux infrastructure and system administration, which gave me a strong foundation in operating systems, networking, storage, virtualization and troubleshooting. Over time I moved into cloud platforms and architecture, primarily AWS and Azure.

As a Cloud Architect, I focus on designing end-to-end cloud solutions rather than only individual services. I work with business and application teams to understand requirements, then design the landing zone, account structure, networking, security, compute, storage, database, HA/DR, monitoring, automation and cost model.

I use Infrastructure as Code such as Terraform and CloudFormation/Bicep, and I work with DevOps and platform teams on CI/CD and Kubernetes-based workloads. I also perform architecture reviews, identify single points of failure, define security and governance standards, and ensure the implementation follows the approved architecture.

My responsibility continues beyond the initial design. I review scalability, resilience, security posture, operational readiness and cost optimization, and I make architectural changes as the business evolves."

One sentence to remember for interviews

Cloud Architect = Requirements + Architecture + Security + Networking + HA/DR + Scalability + Automation + Governance + Cost + Operational Excellence.

And the key distinction:

Cloud Engineer: "How do I implement this?"
Cloud Architect: "Why should we implement it this way, what are the alternatives, what are the risks, and how does it satisfy the business requirement?"
